WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Film Courage: Let’s take that same&nbsp;
student who came to you and said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.240
Zach I want to know how to write&nbsp;
a script that is going to sell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.720
How would you talk to them about working the&nbsp;
craft of writing? How would you guide them?

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:16.080
Zach Zerries, Screenwriter: I would&nbsp;
speak to them asking them again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:23.840
what’s the idea, what’s the pitch? How are&nbsp;
you going to sell this to me because again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.160
screenwriting is selling. You’re not&nbsp;
only representing the product (the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.880
product that you’re writing), you’re&nbsp;
representing yourself in a lot of ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.640
especially when you get out there and you&nbsp;
start pitching and you go on meetings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:49.600
The craft…I mean again I would books like go&nbsp;
read Syd Field, read Blake Snyder’s books, read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.960
William Goldman’s books. Just try to get every&nbsp;
screenwriting book you can and then read scripts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:02.480
read 10-20 scripts before you actually sit&nbsp;
down to start writing, just getting a sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:14.480
to go about it and how again read 10 Oscar-winning&nbsp;
screenplays. Read Tarantino, read Alan Ball, read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:24.640
Brian Helgeland, Robert Towne, Francis&nbsp;
Ford Coppola. I would say just read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:31.440
all of the great scripts. Herman Mankiewicz,&nbsp;
I think it’s Herman who wrote CITIZEN KANE?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.600
I know there were a few Mankiewiczs, I think&nbsp;
it was Herman. There is that new David Fincher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:44.880
movie that is about him [MANK]. Again it goes&nbsp;
back to reference, use those as your reference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.760
Then I would say look do you have an idea?&nbsp;
What’s your idea? What’s your logline?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.640 --> 00:02:03.360
And what is it about that logline that’s fresh?&nbsp;
What is it about that logline that is captivating?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.400
What’s the twist? What is&nbsp;
the irony in that logline?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:15.360
And then from there just sort of repeating what&nbsp;
I said earlier about then taking the next steps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:21.840
plotting, exploring your character, wanting&nbsp;
to know where they begin and where they end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.240
that’s important. Where does your character start?&nbsp;
Is he loathed? Is he loved? By the end is he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:38.800
loved or is he loathed? Boiling it down,&nbsp;
what is his journey? Where are you taking us?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.400
What do you want to say with it? What’s your&nbsp;
theme, most important part, that’s a huge part.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:55.840
What are you trying to say with your writing&nbsp;
thematically. Is it about the evils of war?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:02.160
Maybe that’s the best, there are a lot. Is it&nbsp;
about a guy choosing between love and money?

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:08.480
Film Courage: How far will&nbsp;
you go for a corporation?

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:19.280
Zach: Absolutely. Or again a woman, how far&nbsp;
will she go for her children. I just watched the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:27.760
UNDOING on HBO with Nicole Kidman and not to give&nbsp;
anything away but that definitely touches on that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:38.560
theme. The person that you love not being who they&nbsp;
claim to be or who they are or who you thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:45.360
they were, unraveling them, unraveling the mystery&nbsp;
behind someone. So I think that is something when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:53.760
you set out to write what are you trying to say?&nbsp;
I had a friend of a friend billed it down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:01.840
this - when you’re writing a story what’s the&nbsp;
story about (the logline)? What’s it also about?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:14.160
What’s it really about? Those are definitely&nbsp;
when you set out those are questions that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.320
you answer them and those are the ones that take&nbsp;
time but I think once you answer them and you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:26.320
truthful about what it is and this is&nbsp;
where you see it going again it’s like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:35.920
answering these tough questions early on opens&nbsp;
up the story for you. But that’s what I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:43.040
talking again working smarter not harder. You can&nbsp;
answer again those 15 beats and these additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:49.520
questions earlier on. It’s going to make your&nbsp;
plotting process and your writing far easier and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:57.040
more enjoyable and you won’t be banging&nbsp;
your head agains the wall or worse.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.960
Film Courage: I like that…so What’s it about?&nbsp;
What’s it also about? What’s it really about? Wow.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:08.080
Zach: I remember hearing that probably about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:15.493
15 years ago and it definitely helped me as a&nbsp;
young writer, it definitely opened it up (that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.493 --> 00:05:22.000
was great). What it’s basically saying is&nbsp;
once you have the logline that’s great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:28.080
but what is it you’re trying to say - dude,&nbsp;
what are you really trying to do with this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:34.560
And I think that’s something that people&nbsp;
should be asked and sometimes (oftentimes)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.840
I think people don’t know, they can’t&nbsp;
answer it and it’s like alright well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:46.320
that’s okay but you better well know it before&nbsp;
you sit down and take on the task of writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:53.360
a 90, 100, 110 page script and devoting&nbsp;
your emotion and your time and taking away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.400 --> 00:06:00.080
time from other things to really devote to&nbsp;
this because it is your baby, it is your child.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:08.720
You want to know your child inside-out&nbsp;
right? Do the work, do the smart work.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:12.640
Film Courage: I used to work at&nbsp;
a company that had that banner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:17.840
up [“Work smarter, not harder”] above the&nbsp;
accounting department. And I would sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:24.400
reverse it “Work harder, not smarter.” Oh&nbsp;
no wait? That’s wrong. I would switch it.

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:35.440
Zach: It’s true and again for me it’s&nbsp;
something that came with age and becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:45.360
mature and I think initially we’re impatient and&nbsp;
look, I’m still impatient but with certain things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:52.720
I become much more patient in letting the process&nbsp;
play out. It’s not always easy but in the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:00.400
it definitely lends itself to more&nbsp;
success and more productivity.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.480
Film Courage: If someone came to you and&nbsp;
said I want to learn how to write a script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:07.680
that will sell, what would you tell them?

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:15.840
Zach: Well I would tell them…ask&nbsp;
them what their logline is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:26.400
and then I would really tell then to focus&nbsp;
on where they plan on taking that character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:36.560
Everyone has their own tastes and&nbsp;
everyone sees things in their own way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:46.400
It’s funny back in the day I’ve submitted scripts&nbsp;
to contests and they do wonderfully in one contest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:52.800
and make it to the finals or semi-finals and&nbsp;
then I’d submit that same script to another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:57.840
contest or to a producer and they’d be like you’d&nbsp;
never hear from them or it wouldn’t go anywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:11.040
It is so dependent on the taste of the person&nbsp;
you’re sending it to. I would say what does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:23.760
help and it’s helping nowadays is if writers have&nbsp;
source material (IP - intellectual property). If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:31.840
they have the rights to a comic book, a newspaper&nbsp;
article, so we’re talking about a novella,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:41.440
a podcast (podcasts are being made).&nbsp;
If you own the IP, so many studios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:47.920
and production companies, etc. now&nbsp;
are basing so much of their material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:59.360
on intellectual property that has already been&nbsp;
written or produced or spoken about in the case of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:12.320
audio/podcasts. That is one way to go because&nbsp;
I think if you’ve got a really compelling story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:20.320
and you own the rights to it that definitely&nbsp;
can get a lot of people’s attention. You can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:28.240
say I want to write the script. Again&nbsp;
it varies from producer to studio head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:36.960
but that is definitely a way to help break&nbsp;
in. Again, if it’s a story (something really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:44.800
compelling) or multiple people are after it or you&nbsp;
happen to own the rights, that’s a nice bargaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:53.520
chip that you have and a good way to negotiate&nbsp;
yourself in as a writer. That’s one way to go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:05.600
There’s no sure fire way to come up. I&nbsp;
think if you are just writing it as a spec,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:10.640
if you’ve got a great logline and you have an&nbsp;
intriguing path forward for your character and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:17.600
you send it to a few people who are like yeah,&nbsp;
you’re really onto something here then by all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:22.640
means see it through but as we know there are&nbsp;
no guarantees in the business unfortunately.

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:27.760
Film Courage: Or should the question&nbsp;
to the person be first off before you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:32.080
want to write one that sells, to ask why&nbsp;
you want to write it? I mean it’s not like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:36.240
you’re selling timeshares and hey show&nbsp;
me how to do it? What’s the hot market?

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:50.160
Zach: Right, it goes back to what you want&nbsp;
to write. Right now the Western has come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:56.240
back (let’s just say) and it’s a hot market. I&nbsp;
mean for awhile there it was after GRAVITY right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:04.800
everyone wanted (the studios and the&nbsp;
producers) of all varieties wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:12.320
a great space film. Okay…so I’m sure there were&nbsp;
a lot of people who saw GRAVITY, got inspired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:16.640
up-and-coming writers were like “Yes, I’m&nbsp;
going to write the next great space film.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:20.720
And it very well may have been a great space&nbsp;
film but guess what, the market has moved on?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:32.000
Again, you can’t write for the market. In a&nbsp;
way you have to let the market come to you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:39.520
and that means writing a story that it goes back&nbsp;
to writing a story with compelling characters who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:50.800
you actually give a poop about and you have others&nbsp;
who are invested in them or in their journey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:59.120
that’s what I would say. You ask a&nbsp;
question like why are you writing?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:03.760
What’s the point? Are you in this to make a buck&nbsp;
or are you in this to - do you actually really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:08.720
enjoy the craft and the process and all that and&nbsp;
you really have to. I’m sure there’s some writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:14.560
who’ve just set out to write a script and they’ve&nbsp;
done it and made money but I think the majority of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:20.640
us we really have to be inspired by the type&nbsp;
of story we want to invest 6 months/a year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:25.120
If you’re writing a novel 2, 3, 4, 5 years into.

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:30.560
Film Courage: Sure so DRIVE did well&nbsp;
but if you’re trying to copy that style?

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:35.040
Zach: Yes and I think you always&nbsp;
have to be kind of reinventing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:42.480
yourself. I don’t say reinventing yourself as a&nbsp;
writer but in a way yes because you always have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:49.520
come at it from a different angle and an original&nbsp;
angle. Look again coming back to the space story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:58.160
the market may move on but maybe you have&nbsp;
a unique twist on that space story or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:11.280
on a werewolf story or whatever.&nbsp;
I think it always…how do I say it?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:15.600
You can have the same approach to crafting it&nbsp;
but you have to constantly have a fresh take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:26.000
on different stories and how you not necessarily&nbsp;
want to tell them but fresh takes and twists on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:36.000
your material and what you’re trying to say. I&nbsp;
referenced 1917, not only was that technically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:42.080
well done in my opinion, I just thought that&nbsp;
from a sheer filmmaking standpoint but more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:50.000
important from a character standpoint. It’s&nbsp;
really not a complicated story. He’s trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:59.040
get across France to this guy to tell him that&nbsp;
these men are going to go and it’s life stakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:03.600
and his friend goes along the way and&nbsp;
man we’re invested in these two guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:09.440
these two young guys in the middle of this brutal&nbsp;
war. Oh my gosh! Similar to SAVING PRIVATE RYAN.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:15.280
You don’t have to be a lover of war films to&nbsp;
just be like…I was on the edge of my seat and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:22.560
Sam Mendes is such a great, wonderful director&nbsp;
but wow! And again not an overly complicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:29.520
story but just being invested entirely in the&nbsp;
characters and then getting there in the end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:36.960
It’s like he saves the men and then he’s got to&nbsp;
deliver this news to the lieutenant whose brother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:47.040
he was with who was gone. Oh man, I had&nbsp;
tears streaming down my cheeks but you talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:51.224
about a film that just worked, that stew just&nbsp;
worked brilliantly. Oh many, it was wonderful.

00:14:51.224 --> 00:14:52.400
Film Courage: I like that…a stew. That’s good!

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:59.680
Zach: A stew, a creative stew. But for me&nbsp;
personally I hadn’t experience that in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:07.760
film in awhile and I guess I used to more but&nbsp;
I think it shows where move making has gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:11.840
(studio filmmaking has gone to an extent).

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:13.665
Question for the viewers: What is this video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.665 --> 00:15:16.017
about? What is it also about?&nbsp;
And what is it really about?

